WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.791
Watch what happens when the salt bridge is lifted out. The current stops at once.

00:00:06.391 --> 00:00:12.489
Why? The electrons were happy to travel through the wire. So what was the salt bridge doing?

00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:22.136
In the zinc half-cell, zinc atoms keep forming zinc two plus ions. Positive charge builds up in that solution.

00:00:22.736 --> 00:00:31.740
In the copper half-cell, copper two plus ions are removed as copper forms. The negative nitrate ions are left behind, so negative charge builds up there.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.962
Very quickly, this build-up of charge opposes the flow, and the current stops.

00:00:39.462 --> 00:00:47.203
The salt bridge contains potassium nitrate. Which ions move out of the bridge into the zinc half-cell? Decide now.

00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:58.920
The nitrate ions. They are negative, so they move into the zinc half-cell, where positive charge is building up.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:11.465
At the same time, potassium ions move into the copper half-cell, where copper two plus ions are being removed. And ions from each half-cell move into the bridge too. Ions move both ways.

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:21.127
The salt bridge has two functions. It completes the circuit, and it maintains electrical neutrality in the half-cells.

00:01:21.727 --> 00:01:29.389
When you describe it, name the ions and name the half-cells. Saying that ions flow through the salt bridge earns nothing on its own.

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:41.870
Electrons never enter the salt bridge. They flow only through the wire and the electrodes. In the solutions and in the bridge, the charge is carried by ions.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:52.797
The ions in the bridge must not react with either half-cell. Potassium nitrate is the usual choice, because nitrates do not form precipitates.

00:01:53.397 --> 00:02:04.957
Potassium chloride cannot be used next to a silver half-cell. The chloride ions react with the silver ions to form a white precipitate of silver chloride, which removes silver ions from the cathode half-cell.

00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:14.925
Next to a permanganate half-cell it fails for a different reason. Permanganate is a stronger oxidising agent than chlorine, so it oxidises the chloride ions.

00:02:17.425 --> 00:02:23.131
Open the galvanic cell lab, change the salt bridge, and watch what each choice does to the voltage.
